Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026
Notes: Period live-action storybook fantasy.

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

An Edwardian nursery filled with tactile, handcrafted toys. Their private life unfolds whenever people look away.

A VELVETEEN RABBIT sits atop a Christmas stocking with holly between his paws. He is fat and bunchy, with spotted brown-and-white velveteen, thread whiskers, boot-button eyes, and long ears lined in pink sateen.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

THE BOY, six, with tousled chestnut hair and a round, earnest face, lifts the Rabbit from the stocking. He hugs him, feeds him imaginary almonds, and pulls him around beside a toy engine.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For at least two hours the Boy loved him.

Grown-up hands unwrap a flood of new presents. Tissue paper rustles. The Rabbit disappears beneath ribbons and discarded wrappings.

When the room empties, he lies forgotten on the nursery floor.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Days later, the Rabbit sits among expensive mechanical toys. A clockwork mouse buzzes past him. A painted model boat turns away, displaying its rigging. A jointed wooden lion lifts its nose with official importance.

The Rabbit shrinks against the fender. His small voice is timid and earnest.

Beside him rests the SKIN HORSE, the nursery's oldest toy. His brown hide is bald in patches, his seams show, and only a few hairs remain in his tail. His voice is low, gentle, and truthful.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. That's why it doesn't happen often to people who break easily, or have sharp edges, or who have to be carefully kept.

The Skin Horse's worn face softens into a smile.

SKIN HORSE
Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

The Rabbit studies the Horse's bald coat and frayed tail.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are Real?

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago. But once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The Rabbit touches one perfect thread whisker.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He longed to become Real, and yet the idea of growing shabby and losing his eyes and whiskers was rather sad.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

NANA, a sturdy middle-aged nurse with iron-gray hair, a charcoal dress, and a white apron, searches the bed and cupboards.

The Boy waits beneath the covers. His usual china dog is nowhere to be found.

Nana sees the Velveteen Rabbit and seizes him by one long ear.

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She puts the Rabbit into the Boy's arms. The Boy hugs him so tightly that his velveteen face bulges.

Night after night, the Boy rolls over on him, pushes him beneath the pillow, and clasps him under his warm chin.

The Boy raises the bedclothes into a dark little tunnel.

THE BOY
This is your burrow, Bunny, like the real rabbits live in.

They play in whispers by the glow of the night-light. When the Boy sleeps, the Rabbit nestles against his cheek.

With each passing night, the Rabbit's fur grows shabbier. His tail seam loosens. Kisses rub the pink from his nose.

EXT. GARDEN - SPRING DAY

The Boy pushes the Rabbit in a little wheelbarrow.

They picnic on the lawn. Beneath the raspberry canes, the Boy builds him a fairy hut from twigs, leaves, and flower petals.

The Boy carries the Rabbit everywhere, one arm wrapped firmly around his soft middle.

EXT. GARDEN - DUSK

The Rabbit lies forgotten on the dewy lawn, wet through and caked with soil from the Boy's burrows.

Nana searches by candlelight, finds him, and rubs him roughly with the corner of her apron.

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

She carries him toward the house.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

The Boy sits up and stretches out both hands.

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

Nana places the Rabbit in his arms.

The Rabbit's dull boot-button eyes catch the night-light. For the first time, they seem wise and beautiful.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The nursery magic had happened to him. The Boy himself had said it.

The Boy falls asleep with the Rabbit held against his heart.

EXT. WOOD - JUNE EVENING

The Boy makes the Rabbit a comfortable nest among the bracken, then runs deeper into the trees to gather flowers.

Ants travel between the Rabbit's velvet paws.

Two wild rabbits emerge. BRACKEN is sleek brown with sharp eyes; FERN is smaller and gray-brown. Their soft bodies lengthen and bunch as they move.

Bracken twitches his nose at the motionless toy.

BRACKEN
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

BRACKEN
Ho! It's as easy as anything.

Bracken hops sideways and rises on his hind legs.

BRACKEN
I don't believe you can!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

BRACKEN
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Rabbit looks down. His back is made in one pincushion-shaped piece. He has no hind legs at all.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to!

Bracken stretches his neck and sees the flat seam underneath.

BRACKEN
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

Bracken and Fern laugh.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

BRACKEN
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

Bracken whirls and dances around him. The Rabbit watches with aching envy.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still!

Bracken comes close enough for his whiskers to brush the Rabbit's ear. He sniffs, flattens his ears, and recoils.

BRACKEN
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

The Boy's approaching footsteps crackle through the wood.

Bracken and Fern vanish with a stamp of feet and a flash of white tails.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

Only the ants move. The bracken sways where the wild rabbits disappeared.

The sun sinks. White moths flutter out.

At last, the Boy returns, gathers the Rabbit into his arms, and carries him home.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Weeks later, the Rabbit is old and threadbare. His whiskers are gone, his ear linings are gray, and his brown spots have faded. Only the Boy still sees the splendid rabbit he once was.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
To the Boy he was always beautiful, and that was all that the little Rabbit cared about.

The Boy lies feverish in bed, his face flushed and his chestnut hair damp. He holds the Rabbit against skin so hot it scorches the worn velveteen.

DR. HARPER, a lean physician with silver whiskers and a black frock coat, examines the Boy while Nana tends the night-light.

The Rabbit hides beneath the covers whenever they approach.

Night after night, the flame burns low. Rain taps the windows. The Rabbit remains tucked beside the Boy, never stirring for fear someone will take him away.

Morning light crawls across the bed. The Boy's fever finally breaks.

Later, the Boy sits against his pillows with a picture book. The Rabbit rests close against his side.

INT. NURSERY - MORNING

Bright sunshine pours through open windows.

Beyond the balcony doors, the recovering Boy sits wrapped in a shawl. An open trunk waits nearby for his journey to the seaside.

The Rabbit lies tangled among the stripped bedclothes, listening as Nana and Dr. Harper inspect the sickroom.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
"Hurrah!" thought the little Rabbit. "To-morrow we shall go to the seaside!"

Nana catches sight of his shabby head.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DR. HARPER
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once. What? Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

Nana lifts the Rabbit away from the bed. The Boy does not see her put him into a sack with old books and rubbish.

EXT. BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - DAY

Nana leaves the sack beside a cold bonfire heap at the end of the garden.

A garden fork stands among rows of potatoes and peas. The burning will wait until morning.

The sack has been left untied. The Rabbit wriggles until his worn head emerges from the opening.

Across the garden, the raspberry canes stir in the sunlight.

EXT. BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

Moonlight silvers the garden. The Rabbit shivers inside the sack, his threadbare coat no protection from the cold.

He looks toward the raspberry thicket where he played with the Boy.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He thought of the fairy huts, the quiet evenings in the wood, and the wonderful day when he first knew that he was Real.

A breeze rustles the old books around him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A real tear travels down the Rabbit's shabby velvet nose and falls to the earth.

From that damp spot, an emerald stem pierces the soil. Leaves unfurl. A golden, cup-shaped flower blossoms in the moonlight.

The flower opens.

The NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY steps from it, small enough to stand beside the Rabbit. Her dress glitters with pearl and dewdrops, and fresh flowers crown her hair.

She gathers the Rabbit from the sack and kisses his damp velveteen nose.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

The Rabbit gazes up at her familiar, flower-like face.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

The Fairy holds him close and rises into the moonlit air.

EXT. WOODLAND GLADE - NIGHT

The Fairy alights in a silver glade where wild rabbits dance with their shadows.

Bracken, Fern, and the others gather in a curious ring.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She kisses the Velveteen Rabbit once more and sets him on the grass.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

The Rabbit sits frozen. He watches the others dance and remembers that he has no hind legs.

Something tickles his nose.

Without thinking, he lifts a hind paw and scratches.

He stares at the paw. Strong hind legs now support him. His seams have vanished beneath soft, glossy brown fur, with the faint memory of his old spots showing through. Living ears twitch above long whiskers.

He springs high into the air.

The other rabbits race around him. He leaps sideways, whirls, and bounds across the turf with them.

When he finally stops, the Fairy is gone.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOOD - SPRING DAY

The following spring, the healthy Boy plays among the warm trees.

Bracken and the Real Rabbit creep from the bracken to watch him. The Real Rabbit's faint markings, round black eyes, and soft nose still recall the shabby toy he once was.

The Boy kneels, struck by something familiar.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
"Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!" the Boy thought.

The Real Rabbit holds the Boy's gaze for one quiet moment.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

The Rabbit turns and bounds after Bracken into the sunlit wood.

FADE OUT.

THE END
